5, -0.00295257568359375, -0.019073486328125, -0.0037841796875, 0.023773193359375, 0.0025043487548828125, 0.0092926025390625, 0.024139404296875, 0.0126953125, -0.0037364959716796875, 0.0119781494140625, 0.016693115234375, -0.01317596435546875, -0.01129913330078125, -0.0174713134765625, -0.00925445556640625, -0.0243377685546875, -0.00438690185546875, 0.0187225341796875, -0.00811004638671875, -0.0082550048828125, 0.002349853515625, 0.0012426376342773438, -0.00911712646484375, -0.007625579833984375, 0.0032176971435546875, 0.051727294921875, -0.035552978515625, -0.0111846923828125, -0.006465911865234375, 0.00807952880859375, -0.00482940673828125, 0.016632080078125, 0.0034637451171875, -0.0032520294189453125, 0.0076904296875, -0.038177490234375, -0.0188446044921875, -0.0157470703125, -0.00823211669921875, 0.0252227783203125, -0.0296630859375, 0.0292510986328125, -0.05712890625, 0.0006165504455566406, -0.020599365234375, -0.01221466064453125, 0.0090484619140625, -0.011260986328125, -0.0058441162109375, 0.00681304931640625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "This short work presents a novel extension of the permanental process model to include fixed effects, addressing the need to model both structured random variation and known covariate effects within a unified framework. Specifically, we introduce fixed effects coefficients into the permanental process and place a prior distribution on these coefficients. In the limit where this prior becomes highly diffuse\u2014that is, when the prior variance tends to infinity and the prior becomes non-informative\u2014the resulting model allows the data to determine the fixed effects without prior constraint. \n\nIn this diffuse prior limit, we demonstrate that the intensity function of the permanental process can be characterized using the representer theorem, a result from functional analysis which states that the solution to certain regularized optimization problems in a Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS) can be expressed as a finite linear combination of kernel functions evaluated at the observed data points. This leads to a natural decomposition of the intensity function into two components: a fixed effects term, representing the contribution of known covariates, and a nonparametric term that lies in an RKHS, capturing flexible, data-driven structure.\n\nFurthermore, we show that the limiting equivalent kernel\u2014an object that summarizes the effect of the regularization in the RKHS\u2014defines an RKHS whose squared norm corresponds exactly to the penalty term in the limiting case. This connection provides a clear interpretation of the regularization imposed by the model and clarifies how the complexity of the nonparametric component is controlled.\n\nThis decomposition facilitates straightforward scientific interpretation: the fixed effects term allows for direct assessment of the influence of known covariates, while the RKHS component captures residual structure. For example, in ecological modeling, fixed effects might represent environmental covariates such as temperature or elevation, while the RKHS term models spatial variation not explained by these covariates. Domain knowledge can thus be incorporated by specifying relevant fixed effects, while the nonparametric term adapts to unknown structure in the data.\n\nThe proposed extension broadens the applicability of permanental process models to settings where both fixed and flexible effects are important, such as spatial statistics, genomics, and other fields where interpretable covariate effects and complex dependencies coexist. By clarifying the mathematical structure and interpretability of the model, our approach enables practitioners to more easily incorporate scientific knowledge and to understand the contributions of different model components.\n\nIn summary, our methodological advance consists of extending the permanental process model to include fixed effects with a diffuse prior, deriving the resulting intensity function using the representer theorem, and interpreting the model in terms of an RKHS decomposition with a clear penalty structure. This leads to enhanced interpretability and flexibility, supporting a wide range of scientific applications."}}